    {
      "id": 3,
      "kind": "F_retrieval",
      "payload": "{\"characters\":0,\"documents\":[],\"llm_tokens\":0,\"query\":\"A locality SIMD OpenMP\"}",
      "created": 1792205757.691551
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Produce a standalone quadratic-time polynomial solver. Let phi(x)=x^n-P1*x^(n-1)-...-Pn. Compute r=x^(H-1) modulo phi, then answer sum(r[i]*A[i]) modulo M, with zero-based A. Use MSB-first powering: square/reduce r for each exponent bit, then multiply by x in O(n) when needed, avoiding general polynomial multiplications for set bits. Exploit squaring symmetry and short intermediate degrees. Target O(n^2 log H) time and O(n) workspace. Handle n=1 correctly. Reduce accumulators in proven overflow-safe batches; a whole n-term product sum does not fit uint64. Investigate a rolling direct-recurrence path when H-n is small, using a generic cost-based crossover.\",\"B\":\"Research an independent Bostan–Mori solver with quadratic convolution. Define Q(x)=1-P1*x-...-Pn*x^n and U(x)=(Q(x)*sum(A[i]*x^i)) mod x^n. Extract coefficient H-1 of U/Q. At each step form S=U(x)Q(-x), T=Q(x)Q(-x), retain S coefficients matching the current exponent parity and T's even coefficients, then halve the exponent. Q[0] remains 1, so the final answer is U[0]. Exploit even/odd decomposition to avoid computing discarded coefficients, and exploit symmetry in denominator squaring. Normalize negative residues, reuse compact buffers, and establish a low-overhead serial implementation before considering parallelism. Compare its measured constants against A rather than assuming equivalent asymptotics imply equivalent speed.\",\"C\":\"Investigate exact fast convolution for a standalone Bostan–Mori implementation, targeting larger n. Since M=1000000007 is unsuitable for ordinary radix-two NTT lengths, use suitable auxiliary NTT primes and exact CRT/Garner reconstruction. For canonical input residues, their combined modulus must exceed the convolution bound L*(M-1)^2, where L<=1021; three suitable primes suffice. Reconstruct and reduce modulo M after each convolution, not merely at the final answer. Reuse transforms and buffers, and retain quadratic convolution below a measured size crossover. Prioritize serial performance; test coarse parallelism across independent prime computations only if supported by the unchanged build, using at most four cores. Reject this approach if transform/CRT overhead outweighs its asymptotic advantage within the existing measurements and budget.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only correctness-passing, measured winners from the research trees. Compare complete standalone programs and each proposed combination using the unchanged harness, compiler, target and budgets; gains are not additive. Prefer one main algebraic backend, adding generic small-horizon or convolution-size dispatch only when the integrated program improves. Do not initialize unused backends or retain large matrix objects. Review coefficient ordering, exponent H-1, n=1, H=n+1, zero/sparse coefficients, modular subtraction and accumulator bounds. Preserve the single-integer output and correctness for every valid input. Thresholds may depend on dimensions or legitimate operation counts, never test identities or expected answers. Keep the best measured standalone candidate if integration regresses.\",\"analysis\":\"Only NPbonacci and src/npbonacci.cpp are relevant. Write M=1000000007. The source performs dense O(n^3 log(H-n)) companion-matrix exponentiation, including per-product reductions, a full matrix-by-base multiplication and large matrix storage. The scalar linear recurrence permits O(n^2 log H) polynomial methods, with exact fast convolution offering a further research direction. This run supplies only a correct baseline: four correctness cases and mean runtime 0.01968144 seconds. It provides no candidate comparisons, input-size distribution or hotspot measurements. Consequently, algorithmic improvements are promising but unmeasured; startup, allocation and threading overhead could dominate these short runs. All objectives stay within the fixed four-core ARM64 CPU target and existing evaluation constraints.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"88c8d46239f735983e924b573951639cd295ca1e2465ae06f19dcbd292c9f4b2\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Produce a standalone quadratic-time polynomial solver. Let phi(x)=x^n-P1*x^(n-1)-...-Pn. Compute r=x^(H-1) modulo phi, then answer sum(r[i]*A[i]) modulo M, with zero-based A. Use MSB-first powering: square/reduce r for each exponent bit, then multiply by x in O(n) when needed, avoiding general polynomial multiplications for set bits. Exploit squaring symmetry and short intermediate degrees. Target O(n^2 log H) time and O(n) workspace. Handle n=1 correctly. Reduce accumulators in proven overflow-safe batches; a whole n-term product sum does not fit uint64. Investigate a rolling direct-recurrence path when H-n is small, using a generic cost-based crossover.\",\"B\":\"Research an independent Bostan–Mori solver with quadratic convolution. Define Q(x)=1-P1*x-...-Pn*x^n and U(x)=(Q(x)*sum(A[i]*x^i)) mod x^n. Extract coefficient H-1 of U/Q. At each step form S=U(x)Q(-x), T=Q(x)Q(-x), retain S coefficients matching the current exponent parity and T's even coefficients, then halve the exponent. Q[0] remains 1, so the final answer is U[0]. Exploit even/odd decomposition to avoid computing discarded coefficients, and exploit symmetry in denominator squaring. Normalize negative residues, reuse compact buffers, and establish a low-overhead serial implementation before considering parallelism. Compare its measured constants against A rather than assuming equivalent asymptotics imply equivalent speed.\",\"C\":\"Investigate exact fast convolution for a standalone Bostan–Mori implementation, targeting larger n. Since M=1000000007 is unsuitable for ordinary radix-two NTT lengths, use suitable auxiliary NTT primes and exact CRT/Garner reconstruction. For canonical input residues, their combined modulus must exceed the convolution bound L*(M-1)^2, where L<=1021; three suitable primes suffice. Reconstruct and reduce modulo M after each convolution, not merely at the final answer. Reuse transforms and buffers, and retain quadratic convolution below a measured size crossover. Prioritize serial performance; test coarse parallelism across independent prime computations only if supported by the unchanged build, using at most four cores. Reject this approach if transform/CRT overhead outweighs its asymptotic advantage within the existing measurements and budget.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only correctness-passing, measured winners from the research trees. Compare complete standalone programs and each proposed combination using the unchanged harness, compiler, target and budgets; gains are not additive. Prefer one main algebraic backend, adding generic small-horizon or convolution-size dispatch only when the integrated program improves. Do not initialize unused backends or retain large matrix objects. Review coefficient ordering, exponent H-1, n=1, H=n+1, zero/sparse coefficients, modular subtraction and accumulator bounds. Preserve the single-integer output and correctness for every valid input. Thresholds may depend on dimensions or legitimate operation counts, never test identities or expected answers. Keep the best measured standalone candidate if integration regresses.\",\"analysis\":\"Only NPbonacci and src/npbonacci.cpp are relevant. Write M=1000000007. The source performs dense O(n^3 log(H-n)) companion-matrix exponentiation, including per-product reductions, a full matrix-by-base multiplication and large matrix storage. The scalar linear recurrence permits O(n^2 log H) polynomial methods, with exact fast convolution offering a further research direction. This run supplies only a correct baseline: four correctness cases and mean runtime 0.01968144 seconds. It provides no candidate comparisons, input-size distribution or hotspot measurements. Consequently, algorithmic improvements are promising but unmeasured; startup, allocation and threading overhead could dominate these short runs. All objectives stay within the fixed four-core ARM64 CPU target and existing evaluation constraints.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6.1-sol\",\"nonce\":\"a4b695e9404544b5b75fb483ba781e63\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"88c8d46239f735983e924b573951639cd295ca1e2465ae06f19dcbd292c9f4b2\",\"session_id\":\"a80da638e1054b3a9d13f66e5787f89f\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"5f6f94eef5e40b332bdc9abac7536901c45b6d95b5f1ea5788b6e7bd0c10ad0a\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":11177,\"output_tokens\":6894,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":6051}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6.1-sol\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":18071,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
